<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">sell</ansi>

The <ansi fg="command">sell</ansi> command sells an item to a merchant in
your current room.

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage: </ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">sell <item></ansi>     Sell an item to a merchant.

The command searches your backpack first, then your bandolier,
then your component bag. Quest items cannot be sold.

The price offered depends on the merchant's current stock and
gold reserves. Your <ansi fg="skill">Bartering</ansi> skill can increase
the sale price meaningfully.

Baubles, the small trinkets you sometimes find, are bought only
by general stores and jewellers, at a fixed share of their worth.
Every bauble answers to <ansi fg="command">bauble</ansi>, so
<ansi fg="command">sell all bauble</ansi> sells them all.

<ansi fg="yellow">Stolen goods:</ansi>

A stolen trinket stays "hot" for three days after it is taken,
but only in the town it was taken in. No honest merchant there
will buy it while it is hot; take it somewhere else and it sells
like any trinket. A fence will buy it anywhere, and a fence pays
better for stolen goods than honest merchants pay for anything.
Most towns have one, or have one close by: a shopkeeper who asks
no questions, and like any shopkeeper, only as flush as their
till. Ask around the back alleys,
the rougher taverns and the roads just outside town. Once the
three days are out, anyone who buys trinkets will take it.

Find out more about referring to items by name by typing
<ansi fg="command">help item-names</ansi>.

